---
import Callout from "../../guide/Callout.astro";
import GuidePage from "../../guide/GuidePage.astro";
import Path from "../../guide/Path.astro";
import Task from "../../guide/Task.astro";
import { leavesSite, sitePath } from "../../site/links.ts";
---

<GuidePage slug="video" lead="Episodes play in Katari's built-in player, or in mpv once its add-on is installed.">
  <Task id="player" title="Choose the player">
    <p>
      Each title plays on <strong>Auto</strong> until you choose otherwise. Auto starts in the built-in player. With the
      <a href="#mpv-addon">mpv add-on</a> installed, it moves to mpv when the built-in player can't show the picture, play the audio or
      show the subtitles, when a subtitle has styling only mpv shows as made, or when the source asks for mpv. If mpv can't play a video,
      Auto goes back to the built-in player.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>While an episode plays, tap the settings button at the top.</li>
      <li>Under <strong>Player</strong>, pick <strong>Auto</strong>, <strong>Built-in</strong> or <strong>mpv</strong>.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      The pick applies to every episode of the title. While the title is on Auto, the same row says why it plays where it does, such as
      <strong>Auto is using mpv: the built-in player can't show this video's subtitles.</strong>
    </p>
  </Task>

  <Task id="mpv-addon" title="The mpv add-on">
    <p>
      mpv plays formats and styled subtitles the built-in player can't. It comes as a separate add-on that you install from Katari.
      Katari downloads it from the
      <a href="https://github.com/pa2x2/mpv-android/releases" {...leavesSite()}>pa2x2/mpv-android releases</a> and only uses one signed
      with the same key as itself.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Install:</strong> open <Path>Settings › Players › Anime player</Path> and tap <strong>Install</strong> beside
        <strong>mpv add-on</strong>. When a video needs it, the player also says <strong>This video needs the mpv add-on</strong> with an
        <strong>Install add-on</strong> button.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Update:</strong> when a Katari update needs a newer add-on, the update brings it along and installs it first. When only the
        add-on is behind, Katari offers an <strong>mpv add-on update</strong> of its own. After an update, the settings row reads
        <strong>restart Katari to use it</strong> and has a <strong>Restart</strong> button; until then the previous version keeps playing.
      </li>
      <li><strong>Remove:</strong> tap the delete button beside it on the same screen and confirm in Android's dialog.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Audio and subtitle tracks only mpv can play are listed in the player's settings and marked <strong>mpv</strong>. Picking one moves a
      title on Auto to mpv.
    </p>
  </Task>

  <Task id="servers" title="Pick a server, quality and tracks">
    <p>The player's settings button opens the choices the source and the video offer:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Dub</strong>, <strong>Server</strong>, <strong>Stream</strong> and <strong>Source quality</strong> come from the source.
        Changing one waits for <strong>Apply source</strong>, because the episode has to be loaded again.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Playback quality</strong>, <strong>Playback speed</strong>, <strong>Audio</strong> and <strong>Subtitles</strong> apply as
        soon as you tap them.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Katari remembers the dub, server and stream for the title once the episode starts playing. Until you pick a quality for a title,
      it opens in the one set under <Path>Settings › Players › Anime player › Preferred quality</Path>.
    </p>
    <p>
      When a server fails while an episode opens, the player moves on to the next one the source lists and says so, such as
      <strong>Server 1 didn't respond · Playing Server 2</strong>. It tries up to three. After you pick a server yourself it doesn't switch,
      and the error offers <strong>Try another server</strong> instead.
    </p>
  </Task>

  <Task id="skip" title="Skip intros and credits">
    <p>
      When the source marks an intro, recap, credits or preview, the timeline shows where it is and a <strong>Skip intro</strong>,
      <strong>Skip recap</strong>, <strong>Skip credits</strong> or <strong>Skip preview</strong> button appears while it plays. When
      only credits or a preview are left and another episode follows, the button is <strong>Next episode</strong>.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <Path>Settings › Players › Anime player › Skip intro and credits</Path> chooses <strong>Show a button</strong>,
        <strong>Skip automatically</strong> or <strong>Off</strong>.
      </li>
      <li>
        A title can differ: in the player's settings, <strong>Skip intro and credits</strong> has <strong>Default</strong>,
        <strong>Button</strong>, <strong>Automatic</strong> and <strong>Off</strong>. The row only shows when the episode has marks.
      </li>
      <li>After an automatic skip, the player says <strong>Skipped intro</strong> or similar, with <strong>Undo</strong> to go back.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>An intro the source marks as part of the story is never skipped automatically, though its button still shows.</p>
  </Task>

  <Task id="episodes" title="Filler, stills and summaries">
    <p>Some sources describe their episodes in more detail:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Episodes marked <strong>Filler</strong> carry the label in the list. To hide them, tap the filter button on the title's page and set
        <strong>Filler</strong> on the <strong>Filter</strong> tab. Comics and books call these chapters <strong>Extra</strong>.
      </li>
      <li>
        Episodes can show a still and a short summary. Turn them off with <strong>Previews and summaries</strong> on the same sheet's
        <strong>Display</strong> tab.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>Both settings only show for titles that have such episodes. Continue and the player's next and previous episode skip what the filters hide.</p>
  </Task>

  <Task id="offline" title="Watch offline">
    <p>
      Downloading episodes opens a <strong>Download</strong> sheet. <strong>Quality</strong> chooses between <strong>Best quality</strong>,
      <strong>Balanced</strong> and <strong>Data saving</strong>. <strong>Dub</strong>, <strong>Server</strong>, <strong>Stream</strong>,
      <strong>Subtitles</strong> and <strong>Audio</strong> show when the source offers a choice. <strong>Subtitles</strong> starts on the
      one the player would show; pick <strong>None</strong> to leave them out.
    </p>
    <p>
      Videos that come in several qualities keep only the chosen one, with its audio and subtitles. An interrupted download picks up where it
      stopped. Downloaded episodes keep their intro and credits marks, and play offline in either player.
    </p>
    <Callout label="Can't be downloaded">
      <p>Live streams, copy-protected videos and episodes offered only as torrents.</p>
    </Callout>
  </Task>

  <Task id="extensions" title="Anime extensions">
    <p>
      Anime extensions made for Aniyomi install and work like any other: add their store as in
      <a href={sitePath("guide/first-steps/#extension-store")}>First steps</a>. Extensions that find episodes as torrents show as
      <strong>Unsupported</strong> and can't be installed, because Katari doesn't play torrents.
    </p>
    <p>
      An episode a source offers only as a torrent says <strong>Only offered as a torrent</strong>. Pick another dub or server, or look in the
      source's settings for a streaming option.
    </p>
  </Task>
</GuidePage>
